      <h2 id="two-for-seven">The DQ 2 for $7 deal</h2>
      <p>Some Dairy Queen markets run a <strong>2 for $7</strong> menu, either in place of 2 for $5 or next to it. It works the same way: pick any two eligible items for one price. The items are usually bigger than on the 2 for $5 board, so the per-item value can be similar or better. Because this deal is regional, the eligible items and the price differ between stores.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>How to check:</strong> open the DQ app, choose your store, and look under Deals or Value; or ask at the counter.</li>
        <li><strong>How to choose:</strong> compare each item's regular price at your store with $3.50 (half of $7). Items that normally cost more than that are where you save.</li>
        <li><strong>Which is better, 2 for $5 or 2 for $7?</strong> If your store has both, 2 for $5 is cheaper for snacks and light meals; 2 for $7 is better when you want two full-size items.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For more ways to save, see our <a href="/blog/dairy-queen-app-rewards-hacks">DQ app rewards guide</a> and the full <a href="/combos">combos and deals page</a>.</p>

      <h2>3 Ordering Tips to Get More From Your Meal Deal</h2>
      
      <h3>1. The "Mini Blizzard" Upgrade Protocol (The Best $1.25 You'll Spend)</h3>
      <p>Many diners believe they are locked into the basic soft-serve sundae included with the $7 deal. In reality, DQ’s point-of-sale software includes a standardized substitution modifier. Ask the crew member: <em>"Could I please upgrade the sundae to a Mini Oreo Blizzard?"</em> At 90% of locations, this rings up as an add-on between $1.00 and $1.50. You walk away with a full chicken tender dinner, hot fries, an ice-cold drink, and a world-class <a href="/items/oreo-blizzard">Oreo Blizzard</a> for around $8.25 plus tax.</p>

      <h3>2. The Texas Toast Add-On Tactic</h3>
      <p>The standard $7 Chicken Strip option comes with fries and a drink, but omits the buttery, garlic-toasted Texas toast normally found in the premier <a href="/categories/chicken-strip-baskets">Chicken Strip Baskets</a>. You can add a fresh slice of hot Texas toast for just $0.69 to $0.89. It instantly elevates the value meal to full basket status for less than a dollar.</p>

      <h3>3. Beware the Side-Swap Surcharge Trap</h3>
      <p>While cashiers will gladly substitute your regular fries for <a href="/items/onion-rings">Onion Rings</a> or <a href="/items/cheese-curds">Wisconsin White Cheddar Cheese Curds</a>, be mindful of the POS surcharge. Upgrading to curds typically tacks on $1.79 to $1.99, pushing your $7 meal close to $9.00. Unless you have an intense craving for fried cheddar, stick with the included fries to preserve peak value.</p>

